# Scientific numerics

Assume calculus and linear algebra. Explain UB, races, and lifetime as ways results go silently wrong.

- State **units** and **dtype** (and why that dtype) at the data model.
- Ship a **correctness check**: residual, invariant, manufactured solution, or a property (`isfinite`, conservation to a stated tolerance).
- Name the failure mode (cancellation, stiffness, overflow) when a method is fragile.
- Do not hide `float` vs `double` behind an uncommented typedef.
- Comments: this invariant, this tolerance, this pitfall. README: why this method, alternatives, references.

```cpp
// BAD — magic tolerance, no units
if (err < 1e-6) ...

// GOOD
constexpr double kEnergyTolJ = 1e-8; // J; relative to |E0| ~ 1
if (std::abs(energy_j - energy0_j) > kEnergyTolJ) ...
```
